,"Paolo.Creminelli.1","Martin.Snoager.Sloth.1","Vincent.Desjacques.1","P.Adshead.1","D.J.H.Chung.1","T.Damour.1","R.S.Hill.2","Sheng.Li.2","E.Pajer.1","A.Naruko.1","M.Limon.2","Paul.J.Steinhardt.1","Hai.Ying.Xia.1","Patrick.McDonald.1","D.H.Lyth.1","Yeinzon.Rodriguez.1","J.Khoury.1","F.Iannuzzi.1","Peter.Coles.1","Steven.Weinberg.1","S.Kachru.1","V.F.Mukhanov.1","Xingang.Chen.1","D.Wands.1","D.Larson.2","A.P.S.Yadav.1","K.T.Mahanthappa.1","Xian.Gao.1","G.de.Gasperis.1","W.H.Kinney.1","J.R.Fergusson.1","E.I.Buchbinder.1","A.Albrecht.1","T.Wang.3","Hiranya.Vajramani.Peiris.1","M.C.Wyman.1","Miao.Li.1","Daniel.Baumann.1","M.Sasaki.1","Joanna.Dunkley.1","M.R.Nolta.1","M.Alishahiha.1","Uros.Seljak.1","M.Tegmark.1","D.Marinucci.1","S.Shandera.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"figs_turn.png","caption":"This figure illustrates a model of quasi-single field inflation in terms of turning trajectory. The $\\theta$ direction is the inflationarydirection, with a slow-roll potential. The $\\sigma$ direction denotes the isocurvature direction, which typically has mass of order $H$.","source":"arxiv","key":"574171529c3ca76692281269342d9836","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/574171529c3ca76692281269342d9836"},{"filename":"figs_regimes.png","caption":"The relationship between single field inflation, quasi-single field inflation and multi-field inflation. Here we take inflation evolving two fields as an example. Quasi-single field inflation fills the gap between singlefield inflation and multiple field inflation.","source":"arxiv","key":"f4fc68374e2797afa8f5b38ccfd9d797","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f4fc68374e2797afa8f5b38ccfd9d797"},{"filename":"figs_Fdiagrams.png","caption":"Feynman diagrams for the transfer vertex (a), corrections to the power spectrum from isocurvature modes (b), and theleading bispectrum (c).","source":"arxiv","key":"849b830a404386796c8d1264614dff9c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/849b830a404386796c8d1264614dff9c"},{"filename":"figs_turnphys.png","caption":"The physical interpretation of non-Gaussianity in quasi-single field inflation. The non-Gaussianity in the isocurvature ($\\sigma$) direction(s) is not suppressed by slow roll parameters. This large non-Gaussianity is projected onto the inflationary ($\\theta$) direction if the inflaton trajectoryturns.","source":"arxiv","key":"4727eab305647b2bedb32e5a7bf621f6","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/4727eab305647b2bedb32e5a7bf621f6"},{"filename":"figs_4pt.png","caption":"Feynman diagrams for the scalar-exchangefour-point correlation function (a) and contact-interaction four-point correlation function (b).","source":"arxiv","key":"49a1ed4574fa368851d01d7235b5a0bd","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/49a1ed4574fa368851d01d7235b5a0bd"},{"filename":"figs_Cnu.png","caption":"The coefficient $\\cal C (\\nu)$, defined inEq. \\eqref{cdefinition}.","source":"arxiv","key":"27cd6a6f41865ebcf5e560b4ab054fb9","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/27cd6a6f41865ebcf5e560b4ab054fb9"},{"filename":"figs_nu00.png","caption":"Shapes of bispectra with intermediate forms. We plot $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F$ with $\\nu=0,~ 0.3,~ 0.5,~ 1$. The plot isnormalized such that $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F=1$ for $p_1=p_2=p_3=1$.","source":"arxiv","key":"ec809aa49bbc47daf5b790db5b091bd7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ec809aa49bbc47daf5b790db5b091bd7"},{"filename":"figs_nu05.png","caption":"Shapes of bispectra with intermediate forms. We plot $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F$ with $\\nu=0,~ 0.3,~ 0.5,~ 1$. The plot isnormalized such that $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F=1$ for $p_1=p_2=p_3=1$.","source":"arxiv","key":"f52f3d061615bd3108e95a09322fe192","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f52f3d061615bd3108e95a09322fe192"},{"filename":"figs_nu1.png","caption":"Shapes of bispectra with intermediate forms. We plot $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F$ with $\\nu=0,~ 0.3,~ 0.5,~ 1$. The plot isnormalized such that $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F=1$ for $p_1=p_2=p_3=1$.","source":"arxiv","key":"5984973f20f7d81f840ab16bb490a2a6","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5984973f20f7d81f840ab16bb490a2a6"},{"filename":"figs_eqlimit.png","caption":"The numerical coefficient $\\alpha(\\nu)$ in $f_{NL}^{\\rm int}$.","source":"arxiv","key":"7884f2d04b69e8dc6eff13995cbcafd6","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/7884f2d04b69e8dc6eff13995cbcafd6"},{"filename":"figs_snu.png","caption":"The coefficient $s(\\nu)$, defined inEq. \\eqref{Slimit_quasiloc}.","source":"arxiv","key":"cdb980c0da32b7b9af4713268ffb75c2","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/cdb980c0da32b7b9af4713268ffb75c2"},{"filename":"figs_ansnu03.png","caption":"Shape ansatz \\eqref{ansatz1} with $\\alpha=8$. We plot $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F$ for $\\nu=0,~0.3,~0.5,~1$ respectively. The plot is normalized such that $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F=1$ for $p_1=p_2=p_3=1$.","source":"arxiv","key":"9674cc247d44730b35a750bea9e70238","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/9674cc247d44730b35a750bea9e70238"},{"filename":"figs_ansnu1.png","caption":"Shape ansatz \\eqref{ansatz1} with $\\alpha=8$. We plot $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F$ for $\\nu=0,~0.3,~0.5,~1$ respectively. The plot is normalized such that $(p_1p_2p_3)^2 F=1$ for $p_1=p_2=p_3=1$.","source":"arxiv","key":"11046989c608638799bfa149a268bc42","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/11046989c608638799bfa149a268bc42"},{"filename":"figs_tplnu03.png","caption":"Shape templates \\eqref{template1} with the same convention as in Fig.~\\ref{Fig:ansshapes}.","source":"arxiv","key":"cea4bd67c6f60a3d256895b773ca94b4","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/cea4bd67c6f60a3d256895b773ca94b4"}],"legacy_version":"20160326153812.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Theory-HEP"}],"first_author":{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/013meh722"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/042nb2s44"}],"full_name":"Chen, Xingang","last_name":"Chen","ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1071-6526"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Xingang.Chen.1"}],"first_name":"Xingang","recid":1020404},"control_number":837197,"dois":[{"value":"10.1088/1475-7516/2010/04/027"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Chen:2009zp"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"In quasi-single field inflation models, massive isocurvature modes, that are coupled to the inflaton and have mass of order the Hubble parameter, can have nontrivial impacts on density perturbations, especially non-Gaussianities. We study a simple example of quasi-single field inflation in terms of turning inflaton trajectory. Large bispectra with a one-parameter family of novel shapes arise, lying between the well-known local and equilateral shape. The trispectra can also be very large and its magnitude tNL can be much larger than fNL squared.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["hep-th"],"titles":[{"title":"Quasi-Single Field Inflation and Non-Gaussianities"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Quasi-Single Field Inflation and Non-Gaussianities"}],"curated":true},"created":"2009-11-17T00:00:00+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/837197?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/837197?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/837197?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/837197?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/837197?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/837197?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A837197"}}],"total":1},"links":{"self":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Chen%3A2009zp&size=10&page=1","bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Chen%3A2009zp&size=10&page=1&format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Chen%3A2009zp&size=10&page=1&format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Chen%3A2009zp&size=10&page=1&format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Chen%3A2009zp&size=10&page=1&format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Chen%3A2009zp&size=10&page=1&format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Chen%3A2009zp&size=10&page=1&format=cv"}}